```sql
DROP TABLE users;
```

Same syntax in PostgreSQL and MySQL. PostgreSQL also accepts `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS users;`.

Warning, in plain sentences: this permanently deletes the table and every row in it, and no rollback is possible once committed. Back up the table first (`pg_dump -t users`, or `mysqldump db users`). Check dependent objects before running it: foreign keys will block the drop in both engines unless you add `CASCADE`, and `CASCADE` will drop those dependent constraints and views too.

If you're on PostgreSQL, wrap it in a transaction so you can check the result before committing:

```sql
BEGIN;
DROP TABLE users;
-- inspect, then COMMIT; or ROLLBACK;
```

Confirm you are connected to the production database and not a replica or a shell pointed at staging — `SELECT current_database();` first.
